<worksheet >
    <title>APP-4 Stokes' Theorem and the Divergence Theorem</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>To earn an M on this outcome, you will need to correctly complete all three problems. 
      To earn a P on this outcome, you will need to correctly complete problem 1 and 1 part 
      of problems 2 and 3with progress on the other parts.</p>
  </introduction>  
  <page>
    <exercise workspace="2.5in"><p>Use Stokes's Theorem to evaluate 
      <m>\iint_S \text{curl }\vec{F} \cdot d\vec{S}</m> where 
      <m>\vec{F}(x,y,z)=\langle y e^z, x^2 z, \cos(z^3) \rangle</m> and 
      <m>S</m> is the cone <m>y = \sqrt{x^2 + z^2}, 0 \leq y \leq 3</m>, 
      oriented towards the positive <m>y</m>-axis.</p></exercise>
  </page>
  <page>
    <exercise>
        <introduction><p>Consider the vector field <m>\vec{F}(x,y,z)=\langle x^2, y^2, z^4 \rangle</m>. 
          Let <m>S</m> be the open upper hemisphere <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1</m> and 
          <m>z \geq 0</m>.</p></introduction>
        <task workspace="2.0in"><statement><p>Set up the surface integral that computes the flux of <m>\vec{F}</m> through <m>S</m>.</p></statement></task>
        <task workspace="2.0in"><statement><p>Use the Divergence Theorem to compute the outward flux through <m>S</m>.</p></statement></task>
    </exercise>
    </page>
</worksheet>